 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is memory-bound. Avoids MLP calls at inference. Speedup is >3000x compared to standard NeRF [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** 
-    *   Standard NeRF caching: $O(k^3 l^2)$. For $k=l=1024$, this is ~5600 TB [S1, unofficial].
-    *   FastNeRF caching: $O(k^3 (1 + 3D) + l^2 D)$. For $k=l=1024, D=8$, this is ~54 GB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Renders 300x300 pixel images at 30 fps on Nvidia Tesla V100 in telepresence scenarios (50x faster than NeRF) [S1, unofficial]. Renders high-res novel views at 200Hz on high-end consumer GPUs [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Extremely fast inference; fits on consumer hardware; compatible with NeRF extensions (dynamic scenes, illumination control) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** 
-    *   Aliasing artifacts due to grid caching [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Slower than cached version when not cached (due to larger MLPs) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Mesh complexity grows with volume size; requires downsampling for volumes > $512^3$ [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Quality decreases with lower grid resolutions (pixelated appearance) [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Speed:** FastNeRF is approximately 3000x faster than standard NeRF at comparable perceptual quality [S1, unofficial]. It achieves 200Hz rendering on high-end consumer GPUs [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Memory:** Memory complexity is $O(k^3(1 + 3D) + l^2 D)$. For $k=l=1024$ and $D=8$, dense cache size is ~54 GB, compared to ~5600 TB for standard NeRF caching [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Quality:** Matches or exceeds NeRF quality at high cache resolutions (e.g., $1024^3$). Lower resolutions (e.g., $256^3$) may show "pixelated" appearance or aliasing artifacts due to grid discretization [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Training:** Training time is identical to standard NeRF [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Limitations:** 
+    -   Aliasing artifacts can appear due to caching [S1, unofficial].
+    -   Uncached performance is slower than NeRF due to larger MLPs (384 hidden units) [S1, unofficial].
+    -   Mesh complexity must be managed for large volumes ($>512^3$) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-FastNeRF was introduced by Garbin et al. in 2021 [S1, unofficial]. It was motivated by the need for interactive rates on mobile and mixed reality devices. The method draws inspiration from real-time computer graphics techniques for approximating the rendering equation, specifically spherical harmonics, by factorizing the 5D radiance function into 3D position and 2D direction components [S1, unofficial]. It is used in telepresence, dynamic scene reconstruction, and single-image reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
+FastNeRF was introduced by Garbin et al. in 2021 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the original NeRF architecture [27] by applying a graphics-inspired factorization similar to spherical harmonics in real-time rendering. The method addresses the high computational cost of NeRF, which is compute-bound due to repeated MLP evaluations. By caching the factorized components, it shifts the bottleneck to memory bandwidth, which is more efficiently handled by modern GPUs. It has been applied to telepresence, dynamic scenes, and single-image reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
 
